    "review": "## Summary of Findings\n\nThe code change in `DefaultDriverService.delete` correctly addresses the identified bug by persisting the soft-delete flag using `driverRepository.save(driverDO)` after setting `driverDO.setDeleted(true)`. The modification is minimal, focused, and aligns with the stated acceptance criteria. No style, testing, or architectural issues were found.\n\n## Code Review Details\n\n| File | Description |\n| ---- | ----------- |\n| src/main/java/com/myapp/service/driver/DefaultDriverService.java | The `delete` method now correctly calls `driverRepository.save(driverDO)` to persist the soft-delete flag. This resolves the bug where the deletion was in-memory only. |\n\n## Merge Readiness\n\nThe code change is approved. It directly fixes the identified bug by ensuring the soft-delete flag is persisted to the database, as required by the goal and acceptance criteria. No issues of medium, high, or critical severity were found.\n\n## How to Run the Application\n\nThe provided file `DefaultDriverService.java` is a service class in a Spring Boot application. To compile and run the application:\n\n1. **Compile**: Use Maven to compile the project:\n   ```bash\n   mvn clean compile\n   ```\n\n2. **Run**: Execute the Spring Boot application using Maven:\n   ```bash\n   mvn spring-boot:run\n   ```\n\n3. **Verify**:\n   - Ensure that `DefaultDriverService.delete(Long driverId)` correctly persists the soft-delete flag.\n   - Verify that after calling `delete()`, the driver is no longer returned in active queries (e.g., when querying for non-deleted drivers).\n\n> Note: The exact REST endpoints, HTTP methods, or specific testing procedures for this service are not determinable from the provided repository evidence and were not part of the change requested.",
